# Merge attributes for append-only research ledgers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-08-25). The cloud drain lane strands its output on
# `claude/fire-*` branches until `cc-offload land` brings it home. A full `land --all` pass
# over 113 branches was measured on 2026-08-25: of the branches it reached, 7 landed, 1
# went gate-red, and **16 failed on rebase conflict — every one of them in a single file**,
# `docs/research/venue-foreign-repo-recurrence-2026-08-17.md`. The complement was checked
# explicitly and is the load-bearing half of the diagnosis: ZERO branches that do not touch
# that file failed. The rail is healthy; one document was jamming it.
#
# WHY UNION IS THE CORRECT RESOLUTION AND NOT A SHORTCUT. That document is a chronological
# append log — each cloud session appends a new numbered section ("§ The fifth", "The
# sixth", "the seventh") recording one more occurrence of the same recurring dispatch bug.
# 21 of the stranded branches append to it. Every conflict is therefore two different new
# sections arriving at the same end-of-file hunk, with no competing edit to the same
# sentence: taking BOTH sides is what a human resolving it by hand would do every time.
#
# WHY NOT rerere, WHICH IS ALREADY ENABLED. `rerere.enabled=true` here with 219 recorded
# resolutions, and it does NOT fire on this conflict — it recorded a preimage and stopped.
# rerere replays a conflict it has seen before; each session writes DIFFERENT prose into
# the tail, so every one of the 21 is a first-time conflict with nothing to replay. A
# resolution recorded by hand would clear exactly one branch out of twenty-one.
#
# PROVEN, NOT ASSUMED. Red/green on the real artifact, not a fixture: cherry-picking the
# real failing commit 5b7023912 onto origin/main WITHOUT this file reproduces
# `CONFLICT (content)` in that document (rc=1); with this file the same cherry-pick applies
# clean. The control fails, so the test can fail.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY ONE FILE. `docs/plans/BACKLOG_DRAIN_24_7.md` also conflicted (once)
# and is NOT listed: a plan doc carries structure and accumulated decisions, and this
# repo's INTEGRATE-never-overwrite rule makes a blind concatenation the wrong answer there.
# Union is right for an append-only occurrence log, not for prose that argues with itself.
# Add a file here only after checking it has that same shape.
docs/research/venue-foreign-repo-recurrence-2026-08-17.md merge=union

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Second entry, 2026-09-09. Same shape, same reason, different file.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT. `.claude/rules/agent-operating-lessons.md` is written by EVERY session's
# memory rotor — `bin/cc-memory-rotate:502` routes demotion pointers there precisely because
# that file has no 25,000-char cap. Each session appends in its own worktree and then
# rebase-lands, so every branch collides on the same end-of-file hunk. Pane 629 measured it on
# 2026-09-09: FIVE consecutive rebase conflicts on this one file, and it gave up and DROPPED a
# rotor commit of 26 demotion-pointer stubs — "every session's rotor appends to its tail faster
# than a 20-min gate can close". The rotor already holds a local lock, so this is NOT an
# unlocked-write race: the contention is at the GIT layer, between independently committed
# branches, where no in-process lock can reach.
#
# WHY UNION IS CORRECT HERE AND NOT A SHORTCUT — checked against the rule the block above sets
# ("add a file here only after checking it has that same shape"), not assumed. Over the file's
# ENTIRE history: 58 lines added, ZERO deleted. Every line is either a `- ` pointer bullet or a
# rotor `<!-- routed ... -->` comment, and every write is a `>>` append at EOF
# (cc-memory-rotate:592). Nothing in it ever argues with itself, so concatenating both sides is
# what a human resolving it by hand would do every time — which is exactly what three separate
# briefs written the same night each said by hand, in prose, to their readers.
#
# PROVEN, NOT ASSUMED, AND ON REBASE SPECIFICALLY — the observed failure is a rebase, and a
# driver that fires on `git merge` while missing rebase would silently restore the conflict
# everyone believes is fixed. tests/gitattributes-union-merge.bats asserts BOTH halves in a
# throwaway scratch repo: WITHOUT this line the rebase of two divergent appends exits 1 with
# conflict markers; WITH it the same rebase exits 0 carrying both lines. The control fails, so
# the test can fail.
#
# KNOWN RESIDUAL, ACCEPTED. Union concatenates, so two sessions appending a BYTE-IDENTICAL line
# yield it twice. On an append-only lessons file that is benign and visible; dedupe is
# deliberately not built.
.claude/rules/agent-operating-lessons.md merge=union
